If you've used up your First Prof attempts, or you're close, the past month may have felt like waiting for a verdict. On 15 October 2026, the National Medical Commission gave some of you more room: the **NMC mercy attempt**, one extra try at the First Professional MBBS exam for eligible students admitted from 2019-20 onwards (NMC, 2026).

It's a real second chance, with firm limits. A month on, the rule hasn't changed and some universities are already holding exams.

{% statCallout
   number="5"
   label="Maximum First Prof MBBS attempts for eligible students admitted from 2019-20, counting the usual four (NMC, 2026)." /%}

## What NMC decided

The Commission decided at its 18th meeting on 23 September 2026, and its Undergraduate Medical Education Board issued the public notice on 15 October (NMC, 2026). NMC cites requests from students and universities over COVID-19 disruption, health issues and personal difficulties. It also cites its earlier one-time mercy attempts for the 2019-20 and 2020-21 batches. As of 16 November, NMC's website lists no later notice or clarification on the subject (NMC What's New, 2026).

The core rule: students admitted from AY 2019-20 onwards get **one additional attempt over and above the prescribed four**, so five at most.

## Who qualifies, and who doesn't

The notice grants the extra attempt on three conditions: it is available even past the four-year window, your admission must still be valid, and it must not have been cancelled. It also sets a hard stop for anyone who already used a mercy attempt. The first row below is the basic grant, and the other three rows follow from those conditions and that stop (NMC, 2026).

{% table %}
- **Your situation**
- **Mercy attempt?**
---
- Admitted from AY 2019-20 onwards, First Prof not yet cleared, admission still valid
- Yes, one extra attempt (five in total)
---
- You've crossed the usual four-year window for finishing First Prof
- Still yes, as long as your admission is valid and not cancelled
---
- Your admission has been cancelled by the university or college
- No
---
- You already used a mercy attempt under an earlier NMC decision or a court direction, exhausting five attempts in total (the prescribed four plus one additional)
- No. There is no sixth attempt
{% /table %}

**The 10-year limit doesn't move.** The whole MBBS course, including the Compulsory Rotating Medical Internship (CRMI), still has to be finished within ten years of your admission date. NMC says plainly that the extra attempt does not extend or relax that limit (NMC, 2026).

**It's a one-time measure, not a new rule.** NMC calls it a "one-time exceptional transitional relaxation" for eligible pending cases. It is not a retrospective change to the attempt rules or a precedent for later batches (NMC, 2026). Some coverage summarized it as first-years now getting five attempts (The Hindu, 21 Oct 2026). The notice is narrower, so if you were admitted recently, confirm with your university that it applies to you.

{% callout type="summary" %}
One extra attempt, five maximum, admission must be valid, no sixth attempt for anyone, and the 10-year course limit stays exactly where it was.
{% /callout %}

## The MBBS 4 attempt rule, quickly

Under NMC's guidelines, a student gets no more than four attempts to pass the Phase I exam, within four years. In August 2026 the Kerala High Court held that those four include both regular and supplementary exams, so every **first year MBBS supplementary exam** you sit counts. A paper you didn't appear for at all isn't counted as an attempt, but partial attendance is (The Hindu, 30 Aug 2026).

So the new chance matters most if you're on attempt four or beyond.

## Where universities and exams stand on 16 November

NMC sets the policy. Your university turns it into exam forms, schedules and eligibility lists.

**Kerala.** The Kerala University of Health Sciences (KUHS) issued its circular on 16 October. It restates the NMC conditions and tells college principals to inform eligible students (KUHS circular, 16 Oct 2026). The Hindu reported that Kerala's first-year supplementary exams, first set for 27 October, were pushed back after the NMC order (The Hindu, 21 Oct 2026). KUHS's revised timetable, issued on 23 October, runs 9 to 20 November. Anatomy is on 9 and 11 November, Physiology on 13 and 16 November, and Biochemistry on 18 and 20 November (KUHS timetable, 23 Oct 2026). The timetable is headed "2024 Scheme", so we can't say from it how older-scheme students are scheduled.

**Elsewhere.** KNRUHS's notification list shows a First MBBS supplementary notice dated 29 October and a revised one dated 30 October for December 2026 (KNRUHS, 2026). The listing doesn't say how they handle the mercy attempt, and as of 16 November we found no mercy-attempt circular from a university outside Kerala. Others will use their own calendars and wording.

So check three things with your exam cell now:

- whether your name is on the eligible list
- which exam session your mercy attempt falls in
- how many years you have left before the 10-year limit

## Why the last attempt didn't work

If you've failed First Prof more than once, effort often isn't the issue. The trouble is usually **how** you study. You reread the same notes, memorize answers without the reasoning, then freeze when a viva examiner asks "why?" Re-sitting the same way gets the same result, so treat this attempt as a reset, not a repeat.

Start with your marksheet. Did you fail theory, practicals, or both? One subject or three? An anatomy theory failure and a biochemistry practical failure need different fixes, so don't spend time on parts you already handle.

## A reset plan for anatomy, physiology and biochemistry

**Anatomy.** Draw each region from memory, then check against the atlas. Give real time to osteology, histology slides and spotters, since the practical leans on them. For each structure, ask one clinical question: what happens if this nerve is injured or this artery blocked? Examiners often probe that link, and it makes facts stick.

**Physiology.** This subject rewards mechanism over memory. Turn each topic into a flowchart: stimulus, receptor, pathway, response, what goes wrong. If you can redraw the cardiac cycle or the oxygen dissociation curve and explain every bend, you're ready for theory and viva. Rehearse practicals out loud, step by step, as you would for an examiner.

**Biochemistry.** Pathways feel endless until you ask what each one is *for*. Learn the key regulatory step, then the disease that follows when it fails. That clinical hook does much of the remembering. For practicals, know what each test detects and why the result means what it means, not just the color change.

{% actionBox title="Your mercy attempt reset, step by step" %}
1. Confirm eligibility, exam dates and your 10-year deadline with your college exam cell.
1. Read your marksheet and list exactly which subjects and components you failed.
1. Pull your university's previous question papers and sort questions by topic to see what repeats.
1. Rebuild failed topics around understanding: diagrams for anatomy, mechanisms for physiology, pathways with clinical links for biochemistry.
1. Practice retrieval every day: close the book, write or say the answer, then check.
1. Run mock vivas with a batchmate or senior at least a few times a week.
1. In the final stretch, revise from your own condensed notes. Save new material for after the exam.
{% /actionBox %}

If your sitting is only days away, rank your failed topics by how often they appeared in past papers, and give those the most recall practice first.

## If you're the batchmate

If a friend stayed back while you moved on, share your notes and old papers, offer a 20-minute mock viva, and keep their attempt count out of group chats. If they seem to struggle beyond exam stress, point them gently to your college mentor or counseling support.

## Frequently asked questions

### Does the mercy attempt extend my time to finish MBBS?

No. The ten-year limit from admission, including internship, still applies (NMC, 2026).

### I already had a mercy chance as a 2019-20 or 2020-21 admit. Do I get another?

No. If you've already used a mercy attempt under an earlier NMC decision or a court direction, exhausting five attempts in total (the prescribed four plus one additional), the notice says you are not entitled to any further mercy attempt and rules out a sixth (NMC, 2026).

## The honest summary

The mercy attempt is a genuine break, but a narrow one: one more try, a fixed ten-year ceiling, no sixth chance. Confirm your eligibility first. Then change how you study, not just how long. Understanding the why behind anatomy, physiology and biochemistry gets you through the viva, and you'll lean on it in every phase after this.

